          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Hippocratic Works</strong><br>\nThe collection of texts called the Hippocratic Corpus was attributed to Hippocrates and his followers. Although it is unlikely that he wrote all these himself, the works compile a significant amount of medical knowledge during the Classical Greek period and were used for centuries to teach medical students.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hippocrates's Legacy in Medical Practice</strong><br>\nHippocrates's legacy includes clinical observations, diseases and symptoms documentation, and the therapeutic methods used at the time. His teachings emphasized careful observation and diagnosis, with a holistic approach to treatment focusing on the patient rather than the disease alone.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Theory of Humors</strong><br>\nHippocrates proposed the theory of humors, which stated that the body contains four fluids: blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile. He believed that the balance of these humors kept individuals healthy, and imbalance led to disease and illness.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
